🔑 John the Ripper

🎯 Purpose

John the Ripper (JTR) is an open-source password security auditing and hash-cracking tool. It is used by security professionals to detect weak user passwords, test password strength policy compliance, and recover lost passwords by analyzing stored cryptographic hashes offline.

🧠 Key Concepts

Offline Hash Cracking: Analyzing stored hashes locally without interacting directly with the target service or network.

Hash Formats: Recognizing specific hashing algorithms (e.g., MD5, SHA-256, NTLM, bcrypt) required for processing.

Cracking Modes:

Wordlist / Dictionary Mode: Compares hashes against a list of known common passwords.

Single Crack Mode: Uses user/account metadata (usernames, home directories) to generate targeted password guesses.

Incremental Mode: Systematically checks all possible character combinations (brute-force).

Rules & Mutations: Applying transformations (e.g., toggling case, appending numbers or symbols) to wordlists using custom rule sets.

*Helper Tools (2john): Conversion utilities (e.g., unshadow, zip2john, ssh2john) that extract hashes into formats processable by John the Ripper.

💻 Commands

# Basic Wordlist Attack
john --wordlist=/path/to/wordlist.txt hashfile.txt

# Automatic Format Detection and Cracking
john hashfile.txt

# Specify Hashing Format Explicitly
john --format=NT --wordlist=/usr/share/wordlists/rockyou.txt hashes.txt

# Show Cracked Passwords
john --show hashfile.txt

# Use Rule-Based Wordlist Mutations
john --wordlist=/usr/share/wordlists/rockyou.txt --rules hashfile.txt



📋 Examples

Cracking Linux Passwords (Shadow File):

# Combine /etc/passwd and /etc/shadow into a single file
unshadow /etc/passwd /etc/shadow > unshadowed.txt

# Crack using wordlist
john --wordlist=/usr/share/wordlists/rockyou.txt unshadowed.txt



Cracking Protected Archives or Keys:

# Extract hash from an encrypted ZIP file
zip2john protected.zip > zip.hash

# Crack the extracted hash
john --wordlist=/usr/share/wordlists/rockyou.txt zip.hash



🔎 Investigation

Identify Weak Password Patterns: Evaluate cracked passwords across an organization to identify common weak choices (e.g., seasonal terms, predictable number suffixes).

Identify Outdated Hashing Schemes: Check system configuration files for deprecated hashing methods (e.g., MD5, plain SHA1) that lack modern salt or key-stretching protections.

⚠️ Important

Irreversible Hashing: Cryptographic hashes cannot be "decrypted"; recovery relies on hashing candidate strings and comparing the output values.

Resource Intensive: Brute-force and complex rule-based attacks consume significant CPU/GPU compute cycles.

Authorization: Password auditing must only be performed on hashes legally acquired from systems within an authorized testing scope.

🛡️ Security

Use robust, slow password-hashing algorithms with salt (e.g., Argon2, bcrypt, PBKDF2) to defend against offline dictionary attacks.

Implement strict password length and complexity policies, enforced alongside Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA).

